lesson: 190
title: Who am I? Number riddles
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A riddle about a two-digit number gives facts about its digits; find the digits from the facts and put them together.
explain: «My digits add up to 11 and my tens digit is 3 more than my ones digit.» Take 3 off: 11 - 3 = 8, half is 4, the ones digit; the tens digit is 4 + 3 = 7.
explain: The number is 74. Swapping the digits gives 47, which is 27 smaller: 9 times the difference of the digits.
vars: a=name t=2..9 u=1..8
need: t > u
let: s = t + u
let: d = t - u
answer: 10 * t + u
exercise: I am a two-digit number. My digits add up to {s}, and my tens digit is {d} more than my ones digit. What number am I?
exercise: {a} thinks of a two-digit number. The sum of its digits is {s}. The tens digit is greater than the ones digit by {d}. What is {a.his} number?
exercise: The digits of a two-digit number add up to {s}. The ones digit is {d} less than the tens digit. Find the number.
exercise: A two-digit number has a digit sum of {s}, and its first digit is {d} bigger than its second digit. What is the number?
exercise: Guess my number: it has two digits, they make {s} together, and the difference between the tens digit and the ones digit is {d}, the tens digit being larger.
test: If you swap my two digits, I become {9 * d} smaller. My digits together make {s}. Which two-digit number am I?
test: {a}'s locker number has two digits. Reading it backwards gives a number {9 * d} less, and its digits sum to {s}. What is the locker number?
test: The ones digit of a two-digit number plus {d} gives its tens digit, and the two digits add up to {s}. What is the number?
exercise: What two-digit number am I? My tens digit is {d} more than my ones digit, and my two digits add up to {s}.
exercise: {a} from Texas picks a two-digit number. Its digits have a sum of {s}, and its tens digit exceeds its ones digit by {d}. Which number did {a} pick?
exercise: Find a two-digit number whose digits total {s} and whose ones digit is {d} smaller than its tens digit.
exercise: Two digits, a digit sum of {s}, and the tens digit {d} more than the ones digit. What is the number?
exercise: The number of {a}'s house in Manchester has two digits. Together they make {s}, and the first digit is {d} greater than the second. What is the house number?
exercise: I have two digits. My ones digit is {d} less than my tens digit, and added together my digits give {s.words}. What number am I?
exercise: A two-digit number's tens digit and ones digit add up to {s}. The tens digit is the larger one, and the gap between the digits is {d}. Find the number.
exercise: {a} rides a bus in Toronto whose number has two digits. The digits add up to {s}, and the tens digit is {d} more than the ones digit. What is the bus number?
test: My tens digit take away my ones digit is {d}, and my tens digit plus my ones digit is {s}. Which two-digit number am I?
test: Swap the digits of a two-digit number and it drops by {9 * d}. Its digits total {s}. What was the number?
test: In a quiz in Sydney, {a} is told: «The number has two digits whose sum is {s}; the tens digit beats the ones digit by {d}.» What number should {a} answer?
test: Which number between 10 and 99 has a ones digit {d} below its tens digit and a digit total of {s}?
test: {a}'s team shirt in Auckland has a two-digit number. Adding its digits gives {s}; taking the ones digit from the tens digit gives {d}. What number is on the shirt?
test: A two-digit number becomes {9 * d} less when its digits change places, and its digits add up to {s.words}. Name the number.
exercise: think of a 2 digit number, digits add to {s}, tens is {d} more than ones. whats the number
exercise: {a}'s number got 2 digits, they add up to {s+1}, no wait {s}, n the tens digit is {d} bigger. whats {a.his} number
exercise: riddle on the bus, 3 stops from school: 2-digit number, digit sum {s}, tens minus ones is {d}. number?
exercise: {a} says its {10*u+t}, digits add {s}, tens {d} more than ones. nah thats backwards innit, whats the right number
test: my number's 2 digits, swap em n it gets {9*d} smaller, digits make {s}. which number am i lol
test: two digit num, tens bigger by {d+1} — wait no, by {d} — digits total {s}. what num
test: {a} added em wrong n got {s+d}. the real one: 2-digit number, digits sum {s}, tens beats ones by {d}. what number
exam: A two-digit number is such that the sum of its digits is {s} and its tens digit exceeds its units digit by {d}. Determine the number.
exam: It is given that {a} has selected a two-digit number, the digits of which sum to {s}, the digit in the tens place being greater by {d} than the digit in the units place. Ascertain the number selected.
exam: State the two-digit number whose digits have a sum of {s.words} and a difference, the tens digit less the units digit, of {d.words}.
exam: Whereas the digit occupying the tens place of a certain two-digit number exceeds that occupying the units place by {d}, and the aggregate of the two digits is {s}, identify the said number.
exercise: {a} was a bit economical with the truth: {a.he} said the digits of {a.his} number add up to {s+1}, but really they add up to {s}. The tens digit is {d} more than the ones digit. What is the two-digit number?
exercise: Grandad, who is getting on a bit, had a senior moment: he said the digits of his old house number add up to {s+2}, then remembered it was {s}. The tens digit is {d} bigger than the ones digit. What is the house number?
test: my bro's telling porkies again, says the digits add to {s+3}. nah, it's {s}, i checked. tens digit is {d} more than the ones. what's the number, quick
